<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maritz Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maritz Rebellion was a rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> in 1914–1915 against the Union government's decision to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manie-maritz">Manie Maritz</a>, Christiaan de Wet, Christiaan Beyers and Jan Kemp, some 12,000 rebels took up arms but were defeated by government forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a> by early 1915. One rebel officer, Jopie Fourie, was executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maritz-rebellion.jpg" alt="Maritz Rebellion" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The British Army and The Illustrated London News · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Mushroom_Valley.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Twelve years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> who had fought to the end, the Bittereinders, still hoped to restore the South African Republic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, and some saw a war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, their old supporter, as their opportunity. When war broke out in August 1914, Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a> told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> could defend itself and agreed to invade German South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. Troops under General Henry Lukin and Lieutenant Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manie-maritz">Manie Maritz</a> were mobilised on the border in early September, and another force occupied the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luderitz">Lüderitz</a> on 19 September 1914.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Brigadier-General Christiaan Frederick Beyers, Commandant-General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-defence-force-south-africa">Union Defence Force</a>, resigned on 15 September in protest. That day he set off with General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koos-de-la-rey">Koos de la Rey</a> to visit Major Jan Kemp at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potchefstroom">Potchefstroom</a>, and on the way de la Rey was shot dead by a policeman at a road block set up for the Foster gang; many nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> believed it a government assassination. Maritz then allied himself with the Germans and proclaimed a provisional government of a free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. General de Wet seized Heilbron, and about 12,000 rebels gathered, against some 32,000 government troops, about 20,000 of them Afrikaners.</p><p>Martial law was declared on 12 October 1914. Maritz was defeated on 24 October and took refuge with the Germans, Beyers's commando was dispersed at Commissioners Drift on 28 October, and Beyers drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaal-river">Vaal River</a> on 8 December. De Wet was captured in Bechuanaland on 1 December. Kemp led his commando across the Kalahari Desert, losing 300 of his 800 men on the 1,100 kilometre trek, and surrendered on 4 February 1915.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>At least 124 rebels were killed in battle and at least 229 wounded. The leaders were sentenced to six or seven years in prison and heavy fines, but were released two years later, as Botha valued reconciliation. Jopie Fourie, an officer who had not resigned his commission before joining the rebels and whose men had fired on government troops during a truce, was shot by firing squad at Pretoria Central Prison on 20 December 1914. South African forces went on to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> by July 1915.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maritz rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the First World War began in 1914, Botha’s decision to invade German South West Africa provoked the <b>Maritz Rebellion</b>, a rising of Afrikaner commandos under leaders such as Christiaan de Wet and Manie Maritz, which government forces suppressed by early 1915.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
